  "response": "USS Maine exploded in Havana harbor on the night of February 15, 1898, while lying at anchor on a mission meant to protect American interests during the Cuban insurrection. The explosion killed 260 of the men aboard the Maine. In other words, two hundred sixty members of the Maine's crew lost their lives in the blast, a toll that an American public already primed by sensational press coverage of Spanish rule in Cuba could grasp at once. Because the loss of life was so great, the destruction of the Maine helped push the United States toward war with Spain. As a consequence, the United States declared war on Spain in April 1898, a declaration that came roughly two months after the February explosion in the harbor.\n\nThe question of cause was therefore politically charged from the outset, and the first official answer came quickly. The U.S. Navy convened a court of inquiry led by Captain William T. Sampson to investigate the sinking. Its members heard testimony from surviving officers and relied on divers who worked in the murky water around the wreck. Although the Sampson court of inquiry formally found the Spanish government responsible for laying the mine, it did not assign blame for the explosion to any person or nation. The court's central finding settles this apparent tension. It concluded that the Maine was destroyed by the explosion of a submarine mine. That conclusion concerned mechanism, and the attribution to Spain followed from the mechanism itself rather than from any separate verdict naming a guilty party.\n\nEvery later inquiry has had to confront the same fork in the physical record. Either the explosion that destroyed the Maine originated outside the ship's hull, or it originated inside the ship's hull. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The divers reported bottom plating bent inward near the keel, which favored the first account. By contrast, the heavy fragmentation around the forward compartments lent weight to the second. Spanish naval officers in Havana conducted a parallel examination, but they were denied access to the wreck's interior and had to rely on observations from outside the hull and on harbor witnesses. The Spanish investigation concluded that the explosion originated inside the ship. Yet that conclusion granted a massive detonation without accounting for the inward-bent plating, and so it indicates the external origin of the blast.\n\nThe surviving evidence narrows the possibilities without fully closing them. At least one of the following occurred, perhaps both: a mine detonated against the Maine's hull, or a forward ammunition magazine aboard the Maine detonated. In 1911 engineers built a cofferdam around the wreck and exposed it before it was towed out and scuttled at sea. A second Navy board then examined the hull, moved the presumed location of the external charge, and reaffirmed the mine. The decisive modern reassessment came from outside this tradition of naval boards. Admiral Hyman Rickover published his study of the Maine's destruction in 1956. That is, his 1976 investigation concluded that a spontaneous coal bunker fire igniting an adjacent magazine most likely destroyed the Maine. Rickover's team relied on engineering analysis of the 1911 photographs and on the known hazard of bituminous coal stored beside magazines. Their analysis pointed toward an internal cause.\n\nThe conflicting conclusions thus reflect differences in access, method, and incentive more than differences in the wreckage itself. Sampson's court worked under wartime pressure with limited diving technology. The Spanish inquiry worked from outside a hull it could not enter. The 1911 board worked within an institutional expectation inherited from 1898. Rickover's study applied later metallurgical and ballistic knowledge to the same photographic record. Each of these bodies interpreted the same bent plates and fractured frames, and each read them through the questions it had been asked to answer. For that reason the cause of the Maine's loss remains a matter of considered judgment rather than settled fact.",
